  <scopecontent id="e7b02cbffd37861ee7445cec771baf95">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>Primarily documents Chamberlain's work with the Coordinating Committee on Women's Programs and Task Force on Equal Opportunity and Treatment for Women.</p>  </scopecontent>
  <bioghist id="41d44701d490ae1a58706e49b3fbf0ad">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Mariam Kenosian Chamberlain (1918-2013) was a feminist activist and foundation executive whose work helped establish women's studies programs and women's research centers. Chamberlain worked as a Program Officer for many years at the Ford Foundation.</p><p>Chamberlain first worked at the Ford Foundation as a Program Assistant in its Economic Development and Administration Program. After leaving the Foundation in 1961, she returned to it in 1966 as a Program Officer in its Division of Education and Research, Higher Education and Research Program. She continued working in the Foundation's education related programs until she left the Foundation again in 1981. From 1974 to 1975, she served as the Chairperson for the Co-ordinating Committee for Women's Programs, an internal Ford Foundation committee that sought to assist the Foundation's development of programs and grants related to women.</p><p>After she left the Ford Foundation, Chamberlain joined the Russell Sage Foundation as head of the Task Force on Women in Higher Education. She was also the first President of the National  Council for Research on Women and a founding member of the Institute for Women's Policy Research (IWPR). The IWPR established the Mariam K. Chamberlain Fellowship to honor her.</p><p>Chamberlain earned a Ph.D. in economics from Harvard University.</p>  </bioghist>
